==== Warning ====
By following these instructions you risk bricking your device and voiding the warranty. Just because this worked for me does not mean it will work for you.

==== Overview ====
The hardware appears to be essentially the same as the [[toh:hootoo:tripmate-nano|HooToo TM02/03/04]]. Wingspinner has done some great work to create [[https://github.com/wingspinner/HooToo-Tripmate-HT-TM02-OpenWRT|factory images]] for the HooToo TM02. We will slightly modify wingspinner's HooToo TM02 factory image and instructions and load the resulting image on our RavPower WD02.

==== Requirements ====
  * One vfat formatted microSD card.
  * Linux laptop.
  * Ethernet cable.

==== Instructions ====
=== Create RavPower WD02 Factory Image ===
  - Download wingspinner's factory image [[https://github.com/wingspinner/HooToo-Tripmate-HT-TM02-OpenWRT/blob/master/ramips_openwrt/openwrt-ramips-rt305x-ht-tm02-squashfs-factory-r44945-ws.bin?raw=true|here]]
  - Execute the following command to create the RavPower WD02 factory image.
    * <code>sed -e '3,3s/1649584291/2300554655/' -e '6,6s/HooToo/RAV/' -e '11,11s/TM02/WD02/' openwrt-ramips-rt305x-ht-tm02-squashfs-factory-r44945-ws.bin > openwrt-ramips-rt305x-rav-wd02-squashfs-factory-r44945-ws.bin</code>
  - Execute the following command and verify the command output to ensure that the RavPower WD02 factory image was created correctly.
    * <code>sed -e '3,3s/1649584291/2300554655/' -e '6,6s/HooToo/RAV/' -e '11,11s/TM02/WD02/' openwrt-ramips-rt305x-rav-wd02-squashfs-factory-r44945-ws.bin | tail -n +4 | cksum
2300554655 4933724
</code>
    * **WARNING**: If the verification command output is not **exactly** 2300554655 4933724 then something went wrong and you need to start from the beginning.

=== Flash OpenWrt ===
  - Insert the microSD card into the RavPower WD02.
  - Power up the WD02.
  - Wait for it to boot up as indicated by it showing up as an AP in your wifi scan. Connect to it. The password is "11111111".
  - Once you are connected, open your browser and browse to the IP address 10.10.10.254 . The RavPower GUI should appear. Bypass the setup wizard by closing its popup window.
  - Make sure the microSD card is properly mounted by clicking on the "Explore" menu item and viewing the directory of the microSD card.
  - Now go to the "System" menu, select the firmware upgrade menu item, and then upgrade the WD02 using the "openwrt-ramips-rt305x-rav-wd02-squashfs-factory-r44945-ws.bin" you created. 
  - **WAIT** until the timer popup times out before powering down the WD02 as it takes time to write and verify the entire on-board flash MTD.
  - Connect an ethernet cable between your laptop and the WD02. Set your laptop for DHCP (probably is by default) and then power-on the WD02. If the upgrade worked, you'll see all the LEDs light up. That's the new UBoot. When it starts to boot the kernel they will go out. After about 30 seconds it should be booted into OpenWrt.
  - [[docs:guide-user:luci:luci.essentials|LuCI]] should be up and running so browse to 192.168.1.1 and configure as needed. This install uses the Chaos Calmer snapshot r44945.

=== Upgrade to Released Chaos Calmer 15.05.1 ===
Once you have OpenWrt on your device, you can use the OpenWrt [[docs:guide-user:installation:generic.sysupgrade|sysupgrade]] mechanism to upgrade to the released Chaos Calmer 15.05.1 by using the HooToo TM02 sysupgrade image [[https://downloads.openwrt.org/chaos_calmer/15.05.1/ramips/rt305x/openwrt-15.05.1-ramips-rt305x-ht-tm02-squashfs-sysupgrade.bin|here]]. Then you can install the packages necessary to use your microSD card as [[docs:guide-user:additional-software:extroot_configuration]] and then re-install [[docs:guide-user:luci:luci.essentials|LuCI]] and any other packages - even gcc.

